Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good set of questions to bring to your annual. First, a couple quick clarifying questions so my recommendations are accurate:

- Can you confirm your current medications and doses (you listed metformin and metoprolol but their indications looked swapped)?  
- When you wrote “My last blood pressure was 28” — do you mean 128 (systolic) or is that a different reading (please give the full BP if you have it)?  
- Have you had a DEXA (bone density) scan before? If so, when and what were the results (T-scores)?  
- How well controlled are your GERD symptoms on current therapy? Any alarm symptoms (weight loss, difficulty swallowing, recurrent vomiting, GI bleeding, anemia)?

Below are high-level, non-diagnostic recommendations and the key questions to ask your PCP (Dr. David Nguyen) about osteopenia and whether you need a referral to GI. This is general information — your PCP will apply it to your specific health status.

Osteopenia — what to consider and what to ask
- Tests to check or update
  - DEXA (bone density) if you haven’t had one in the last 1–3 years (frequency depends on T-score & risk).  
  - Laboratory work to look for secondary causes: serum calcium, 25‑OH vitamin D, TSH, creatinine/renal function, CBC, and possibly serum protein electrophoresis or parathyroid tests if indicated.  
  - FRAX (10‑year fracture risk) assessment — ask if they’ll calculate this for you.  
- When to treat vs monitor
  - Osteopenia (T-score between −1.0 and −2.5) is often managed with lifestyle and supplementation unless FRAX risk is high or you have additional risk factors — ask your PCP whether you meet treatment thresholds (e.g., high FRAX risk or progressive bone loss).  
  - If bone loss is rapid or you have prior fragility fractures, medication (bisphosphonates or other agents) may be considered.  
- Lifestyle and supplements to discuss
  - Calcium intake 1,000–1,200 mg/day (food + supplements as needed) and vitamin D (often 800–1000 IU/day; check vitamin D level first).  
  - Weight‑bearing and resistance exercise, fall-risk mitigation, smoking cessation, limiting heavy alcohol.  
- Practical questions to ask Dr. Nguyen
  - “Do I need a DEXA now? If so, how often should we repeat it?”  
  - “Can you calculate my FRAX score and explain whether I meet criteria for pharmacologic therapy?”  
  - “Which labs would you like prior to deciding on treatment?”  
  - “If treatment is recommended, what are the options and risks/benefits given my heart-failure history and other meds?”

GI referral (Boston GI) — when it’s appropriate
- When referral/upper endoscopy (EGD) is commonly recommended
  - Alarm symptoms: dysphagia (trouble swallowing), unintentional weight loss, GI bleeding/black stools, persistent vomiting, or iron-deficiency anemia → refer for urgent evaluation/EGD.  
