I can help — this is a good set of issues to prepare for your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions first, then I’ll give a focused set of questions to ask Dr. Nguyen, what tests to request, and what to ask/expect if you end up referring to Boston GI.

Clarifying questions
- Can you confirm your sex and whether you’re pre- or post-menopausal? (Osteopenia/osteoporosis evaluation changes by sex and menopausal status.)
- Please confirm your current medications and doses (you wrote “hypertension (metformin 500 mg twice daily) and GERD (metoprolol 25 mg)” — metformin is usually for diabetes and metoprolol for blood pressure; I want to be sure I have the right meds listed).
- Did you mean your last blood pressure was 128 (or something similar)? “28” would be dangerously low — please confirm.
- Any prior fragility fractures (wrist, hip, vertebral fracture after minimal trauma)?
- Any current steroid use, smoking, heavy alcohol use (≥3 drinks/day), or family history of hip fracture?
- Any recent DEXA scan (bone density)? If yes, when and what were the T-scores?

What to ask your PCP (Dr. David Nguyen) — osteopenia management
- “Given my age (41) and my bone density status (if you have my DEXA results, mention them), do I meet criteria for pharmacologic therapy or should we observe and optimize non-drug measures?”
- “Please order a DEXA scan now if I don’t have one within the last 1–2 years. If I do, ask whether a repeat DEXA is indicated and when.”
- “Please calculate a FRAX score (10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture and hip fracture) and interpret it for me.” (Note: treatment is generally recommended for osteopenia if FRAX hip ≥3% or major fracture ≥20% in many guidelines.)
- “Which labs should we check to rule out secondary causes of low bone density?” — suggest: CBC, CMP (calcium, albumin, creatinine), 25-hydroxy vitamin D, TSH, PTH if indicated, magnesium, phosphorus, and possibly testosterone in men or estradiol in women if clinically indicated.
- “Do I need referral to endocrinology or bone specialist if FRAX is high or if labs are abnormal?”
- “Should I start calcium and vitamin D supplementation now? If so, what doses? (Commonly 1,000–1,200 mg elemental calcium/day and vitamin D 800–2,000 IU/day depending on level and deficiency.)”
- “Would you recommend starting pharmacologic treatment (e.g., oral bisphosphonate like alendronate)? If so, what are the risks/benefits for me given my heart-failure history and meds?”
- “Do I need a dental evaluation before starting bisphosphonates? Any precautions given dental health or planned dental procedures?”
- “How often should we recheck bone density if we observe (vs treat) — typically every 1–2 years?”

Practical labs/tests to request at the visit (osteopenia focus)
- DEXA scan (lumbar spine and hip) or confirm last date/results
- FRAX calculation
- 25-hydroxy vitamin D
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, calcium, phosphorus)
- CBC (if concerned about secondary causes)
- TSH
- Consider PTH and testosterone (if relevant) if lab results or clinical context suggest

Lifestyle and non-drug management points to discuss
- Weight-bearing and resistance exercise program
- Smoking cessation and limiting alcohol (≤1–2 drinks/day)
- Fall risk assessment and home safety if indicated
- Ensure adequate dietary calcium; supplement to reach 1,000–1,200 mg/day if diet insufficient
- Vitamin D repletion if deficient (dose guided by level)

When to consider pharmacologic therapy (high-level criteria)
- T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis)
- Prior fragility fracture
- Osteopenia (T-score −1.0 to −2.5) PLUS high FRAX risk (hip fracture ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture ≥20% over 10 years) — discuss guideline thresholds with your PCP
- If treated, common first-line: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate or risedronate); alternatives include IV bisphosphonate or denosumab depending on comorbidities and renal function

Special considerations with heart failure
- If you take loop diuretics (e.g., furosemide), they can increase calcium excretion and may affect bone health — mention this to your PCP.
- Renal function matters for some osteoporosis medications (e.g., bisphosphonates). Make sure recent creatinine/eGFR are available before starting certain agents.
- Coordinate medication lists and possible interactions with your cardiology/PCP.

Boston GI — when to refer and what to ask
Consider referral to GI/endoscopy if any of the following apply:
- “Alarm” symptoms: difficulty swallowing (dysphagia), painful swallowing (odynophagia), unintentional weight loss, GI bleeding, persistent vomiting, or iron-deficiency anemia.
- Persistent GERD symptoms despite an adequate trial of optimized PPI therapy (usually 8 weeks) or if you want evaluation for Barrett’s esophagus because of chronic GERD and other risk factors.
- High-risk profile for Barrett’s: long-standing GERD, male sex, white race, age >50 (age >50 increases risk — you are 41 so age alone is not an indication), central obesity, smoking, family history.

Questions to ask Boston GI if referred
- “Do I need upper endoscopy (EGD) now or only if I have alarm symptoms or fail PPI therapy?”
- “Given my age (41), heart-failure history, and current meds, am I at elevated risk for Barrett’s that would justify endoscopy?”
- “If we do endoscopy and find reflux-related changes, what are the treatment and surveillance recommendations for someone my age?”
- “If symptoms persist despite PPI therapy, would you recommend ambulatory pH monitoring or surgical options (e.g., fundoplication or endoscopic treatment)?”

Immediate actions you can take before the visit
- Verify and write down your exact current meds and doses (including over-the-counter calcium/vitamin D), allergies, and any recent labs or DEXA results.
- Confirm the true BP reading (if you meant 128 or something else; if systolic or diastolic is extremely low or high, seek urgent care).
- If you don’t have a recent DEXA, ask to have one ordered before or at the visit.
