I can help — this is a good set of things to prepare before your annual. A few quick clarifying questions first so my recommendations and any draft message to Dr. Nguyen are accurate:
- Can you confirm your sex/gender (some osteoporosis decisions differ)?  
- Please double-check your medication list and which medication is for which condition. (You wrote “hypertension (metformin 500 mg twice daily), GERD (metoprolol 25 mg)” — metformin is usually for diabetes and metoprolol is usually for blood pressure/heart; I want to list them correctly in any message to your PCP.)  
- Your last blood pressure reading “28” looks incomplete — can you confirm the full reading (e.g., 128/78)?

Below are concise, practical points to think through and specific questions you can ask Dr. Nguyen about osteopenia and about whether you need a GI referral to Boston GI.

Osteopenia — what matters and questions to ask
- Key factors that affect management: your T-score from the most recent DEXA, age, sex, prior fractures, family history of hip fracture, smoking, alcohol, steroid use, low BMI, and secondary causes (thyroid disease, malabsorption, low vitamin D, hypogonadism, etc.).  
- Tests your PCP may order: DEXA bone density (if not done recently), 25‑OH vitamin D, serum calcium, creatinine, TSH, basic metabolic panel, and possibly PTH or celiac testing if indicated. Consider FRAX 10‑year fracture risk calculation (usable from age 40+) to guide treatment decisions.  
- When medications are recommended: Generally pharmacologic treatment is more strongly considered if T-score ≤ -2.5 (osteoporosis) or if osteopenia (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5) plus elevated FRAX risk (commonly used thresholds: 10‑yr major osteoporotic fracture ≥20% or hip fracture ≥3%) or prior fragility fracture. For younger patients (like age 41) the threshold to start drugs is usually higher and secondary causes are often evaluated first.  
- Lifestyle and supplements: sufficient calcium and vitamin D, weight-bearing/resistance exercise, fall prevention, limit alcohol, stop smoking. Your PCP can advise target vitamin D level and dose.  
- Safety/other concerns: some bone drugs have renal or other considerations; discuss kidney function and heart-failure implications with your PCP.  
Suggested questions to ask Dr. Nguyen about osteopenia:
  - “Do I need a DEXA scan now (and when was my last one)? Can you review the T-score and calculate my FRAX risk with my current data?”  
  - “Should we check labs for secondary causes (25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, TSH, CMP, PTH if needed)?”  
  - “Given my age (41), T-score of X (if you know it), and my other health history, do you recommend starting medication (bisphosphonate, denosumab, etc.) now or try lifestyle/supplementation first?”  
  - “If medication is recommended, what are the pros/cons given my heart-failure history and current meds?”  
  - “How often should we repeat DEXA if we don’t start meds?”

GERD and GI referral — what to consider
- When to refer to GI/endoscopy: alarm symptoms (dysphagia, odynophagia, GI bleeding, iron‑deficiency anemia, unexplained weight loss, persistent vomiting), failure of appropriate PPI therapy, or long-standing GERD with risk factors for Barrett’s esophagus (chronic symptoms >5–10 years, male sex, age >50, central obesity). At age 41 without alarm features, initial management often is medical (PPI) and lifestyle changes; endoscopy usually not routine unless symptoms are refractory or alarm features present.  
- PPI use and bone risk: long-term PPI use has been associated with small increased fracture risk in some studies — discuss this with your provider if you’re on chronic PPI therapy.  
Suggested questions to ask Dr. Nguyen about your GERD and referral:
  - “Are my GERD symptoms controlled on my current medication? (Please note: tell me if you are on a PPI — which one and how long.)”  
  - “Do I have any alarm symptoms that should trigger an immediate referral for endoscopy?”  
  - “Given my age and heart history, do you recommend referral to Boston GI now or only if symptoms continue despite optimized therapy?”  
  - “If a referral is recommended, will Boston GI need prior testing (labs, H. pylori test) or should we try a PPI trial first?”

Other items to prepare for the visit
- Bring/confirm: accurate med list with doses and what each med is for, current blood pressure readings (home measurements over a week are helpful), any prior DEXA reports, recent labs (A1c, lipid panel, kidney function), and a list of symptoms (GERD frequency, any alarm features, falls, fractures).  
- Routine checks especially given your history: BP check and plan for hypertension management, review heart-failure status and meds, A1c if you’re on metformin, kidney function, lipid panel, immunizations, and medication interactions.

Next steps I can help with
- If you want, I can draft an email to Dr. David Nguyen at Phoenix Family Medicine summarizing these questions and asking which tests or referrals he recommends. Tell me if you want it sent now or saved as a draft and confirm the meds and BP reading first.  
- I can also create a one-page checklist you can take to your appointment.

