Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good set of things to prepare before your annual. A few quick clarifying questions, then practical suggestions and sample questions you can ask Dr. Nguyen (or I can draft an email to him asking for the tests/referral).

Quick clarifications
- You listed meds that don’t match the conditions (you wrote “hypertension (metformin 500 mg twice daily)” and “GERD (metoprolol 25 mg)”). Can you confirm which medication you actually take for each condition (names + doses)?
- You said “my last blood pressure was 28” — can you confirm that reading (systolic/diastolic)? 28 is likely incomplete/mistyped.
- Are you on a proton pump inhibitor (omeprazole, pantoprazole, esomeprazole) or H2 blocker for GERD, and how long? Any alarm symptoms (difficulty swallowing, weight loss, bleeding/black stools, recurrent vomiting)?
- Any past fractures as an adult, family history of osteoporosis/hip fractures, long-term steroid use, smoking, heavy alcohol use, low body weight, or other medical problems (thyroid disease, celiac disease, chronic kidney disease)?
- Sex and menopausal status (if relevant) — that affects fracture risk and testing.

Osteopenia: what to consider before your visit
- Risk assessment:
  - Get a DEXA (bone density) scan if you haven’t had one recently. At 41 a DEXA is not routine unless risk factors or prior low bone density — but you should discuss because you mention osteopenia.
  - Calculate FRAX (10-year fracture risk) if DEXA is low; that helps decide treatment.
- Labs to check for secondary causes (reasonable to request):
  - 25-OH vitamin D
  - Basic metabolic panel / calcium
  - TSH
  - CBC
  - Liver function tests
  - Sex hormones if indicated (testosterone in men, estradiol in some women)
  - Celiac serology if risk factors or malabsorption
  - Consider 24-hr urinary calcium (if abnormal calcium or question of hypercalciuria)
  - If on loop diuretics or chronic steroids, mention that to your PCP (these can affect bone).
- Lifestyle and supplements:
  - Weight-bearing and resistance exercise
  - Adequate calcium intake (diet + supplements to target ~1,000–1,200 mg/day depending on diet; discuss exact target with PCP)
  - Vitamin D supplementation guided by level (typical maintenance 800–2000 IU/day; treat deficiency per guidelines)
  - Smoking cessation, limit alcohol, fall-prevention strategies
- When medication is considered:
  - For osteopenia at age 41, pharmacologic therapy (bisphosphonates, raloxifene, etc.) is generally reserved for high fracture risk, rapid bone loss, or secondary causes — your PCP may refer to endocrinology/osteoporosis specialist if they’re considering long-term medication.
  - If you have progressive bone loss or a fragility fracture, specialist referral and medication are more likely.

GI referral (Boston GI) — when it’s appropriate
- Common reasons to refer for upper endoscopy (EGD) or GI evaluation:
  - “Alarm” symptoms: dysphagia (trouble swallowing), odynophagia (painful swallowing), unintentional weight loss, GI bleeding or anemia, persistent vomiting
  - Refractory GERD despite an adequate trial of PPI therapy
