Below are suggested items to cover during the visit with Dr. David Nguyen, plus tests and criteria for referral to gastroenterology (Boston GI) and practical osteopenia management.

A. Things to clarify/review with your PCP at the visit
- Medication reconciliation: confirm all current meds (names, doses, frequency) including antihypertensives, heart-failure meds, diabetes meds, acid-suppressing meds (PPI/H2), supplements (calcium, vitamin D), steroids, or other bone-affecting drugs.
- Blood pressure: review recent home and clinic readings; bring a home BP log if available. If readings look abnormal, ask whether med adjustments or ambulatory BP monitoring are needed.
- Heart-failure status: review symptoms (edema, dyspnea, exercise tolerance), current NYHA status, last echo (EF), and whether BNP or cardiology follow-up is due.
- Diabetes/metabolic: if you are on metformin, ask for A1c and fasting glucose if not done recently.
- GERD symptoms: duration, frequency, current therapy (PPI, H2 blocker), response to therapy, and any alarm symptoms (see below).
- Osteopenia: ask whether a DEXA is indicated/when the last one was done, and whether your fracture risk (FRAX) has been calculated.

B. Tests and assessments your PCP may order (useful to request if not already recent)
- DEXA scan (hip and lumbar spine) — to quantify bone density and get a T-score (if you haven’t had one recently).
- FRAX calculation (uses DEXA plus clinical factors) to estimate 10-year fracture risk.
- Labs to look for secondary causes of low bone density or reversible contributors:
  - Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D
  - Serum calcium, albumin (or corrected calcium)
  - Basic metabolic panel (kidney function, electrolytes)
  - TSH
  - CBC (look for anemia)
  - Testosterone (in men) or estradiol (in women) if clinically indicated
  - Celiac serology if malabsorption suspected
  - Consider 24-hour urinary calcium or spot urine Ca/Cr if lab panel suggests
  - A1c and lipid panel if diabetes/hyperlipidemia monitoring needed
- If heart-failure surveillance is due: BNP, echocardiogram, or cardiology follow-up as appropriate.

C. Osteopenia management points to discuss
- Confirm diagnosis and risk: DEXA T-score defines osteopenia (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5). Treatment decisions depend on age, sex, and FRAX 10-year risk thresholds (commonly: consider pharmacologic therapy if hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture ≥20% in many guidelines).
- Non-drug measures to start for most patients:
  - Weight-bearing and resistance exercise program
  - Smoking cessation and limiting excessive alcohol
  - Adequate calcium intake (diet first; total typically ~1,000–1,200 mg/day depending on age/sex) and vitamin D supplementation (commonly 800–2000 IU/day guided by levels)
  - Fall-risk assessment and home-safety measures
- When to consider medication:
  - If DEXA shows osteoporosis (T-score ≤ -2.5) OR FRAX risk above treatment thresholds OR prior fragility fracture — discuss antiresorptive therapy (oral bisphosphonate like alendronate, IV zoledronic acid, or alternatives).
